Sure, here's an example implementation of a priority queue in Java using a linked list and a custom sorting algorithm based on the urgency level of each task:
```java
public class Task {
    private String description;
    private int priority;
    private int urgency;
    public Task(String description, int priority, int urgency) {
        this.description = description;
        this.priority = priority;
        this.urgency = urgency;
    }
    public String getDescription() {
        return description;
    }
    public int getPriority() {
        return priority;
    }
    public int getUrgency() {
        return urgency;
    }
}
public class PriorityQueue {
    private LinkedList<Task> tasks;
    public PriorityQueue() {
        tasks = new LinkedList<>();
    }
    public void addTask(Task task) {
        if (tasks.isEmpty()) {
            tasks.add(task);
            return;
        }
        int i;
        for (i = 0; i < tasks.size(); i++) {
            if (task.getUrgency() > tasks.get(i).getUrgency()) {
                break;
            } else if (task.getUrgency() == tasks.get(i).getUrgency() &&
                       task.getPriority() > tasks.get(i).getPriority()) {
                break;
            }
        }
        tasks.add(i, task);
    }
    public Task getNextTask() {
        return tasks.poll();
    }
    public boolean isEmpty() {
        return tasks.isEmpty();
    }
}
```
In this implementation, the `Task` class has three fields: `description`, `priority`, and `urgency`. The `PriorityQueue` class uses a linked list to store the tasks and provides three methods: `addTask`, `getNextTask`, and `isEmpty`.
The `addTask` method adds a new task to the queue by iterating through the list and finding the correct position based on the urgency and priority of the task. The `getNextTask` method removes and returns the next task in the queue, and the `isEmpty` method checks if the queue is empty.
Note that this implementation assumes that higher values of urgency and priority indicate more urgent and important tasks, respectively. If the opposite is true, the comparison operators in the `addTask` method should be reversed.

