```
using System;
class Program
{
    static void Main(string[] args)
    {
        double a = 5;
        double b = 10;
        double c = 12;
        // Use the Pythagorean theorem to find the height of the triangle
        double s = (a + b + c) / 2;
        double height = 2 * Math.Sqrt(s * (s - a) * (s - b) * (s - c)) / b;
        // Calculate the maximum area of the triangle
        double area = 0.5 * b * height;
        Console.WriteLine("The maximum area of the triangle is: " + area);
    }
}
```
In this code, we first define the lengths of the sides of the triangle as `a`, `b`, and `c`. We then use the Pythagorean theorem to find the height of the triangle, which is the distance between the base (side `b`) and the opposite vertex. Finally, we calculate the maximum area of the triangle by multiplying the base (`b`) by the height and dividing by 2. The result is printed to the console using `Console.WriteLine()`.

