Creating a nonverbal communication system that conveys complex ideas or subtle emotions is a challenging task, and there are many ways to approach it. One possible approach is to use C++ to create a graphical user interface (GUI) that incorporates visual, auditory, and tactile elements to convey meaning.
Here is a code example that demonstrates how to create a simple GUI using C++ and the Qt framework. This example uses a slider to convey a range of emotions, from happy to sad, and displays a corresponding emoticon.
```
#include <QtWidgets>
int main(int argc, char *argv[])
{
    QApplication app(argc, argv);
    // Create a main window
    QMainWindow window;
    window.setWindowTitle("Emotion Slider");
    // Create a slider
    QSlider *slider = new QSlider(Qt::Horizontal);
    slider->setRange(0, 100);
    // Create a label to display the emoticon
    QLabel *label = new QLabel;
    label->setAlignment(Qt::AlignCenter);
    label->setPixmap(QPixmap(":/emoticons/happy.png"));
    // Connect the slider valueChanged signal to the label pixmap slot
    QObject::connect(slider, &QSlider::valueChanged, [&]() {
        int value = slider->value();
        if (value < 25) {
            label->setPixmap(QPixmap(":/emoticons/sad.png"));
        } else if (value < 50) {
            label->setPixmap(QPixmap(":/emoticons/neutral.png"));
        } else if (value < 75) {
            label->setPixmap(QPixmap(":/emoticons/happy.png"));
        } else {
            label->setPixmap(QPixmap(":/emoticons/ecstatic.png"));
        }
    });
    // Add the slider and label to a layout
    QVBoxLayout *layout = new QVBoxLayout;
    layout->addWidget(slider);
    layout->addWidget(label);
    // Set the main window layout
    QWidget *widget = new QWidget;
    widget->setLayout(layout);
    window.setCentralWidget(widget);
    // Show the main window
    window.show();
    return app.exec();
}
```
In this example, we create a main window, a slider, and a label to display the emoticon. We then connect the slider valueChanged signal to a lambda function that updates the label pixmap based on the slider value. Finally, we add the slider and label to a layout and set it as the main window layout.
This is just a simple example, but it demonstrates how C++ can be used to create a nonverbal communication system that incorporates visual, auditory, and tactile elements to convey meaning.

