How can Go be used to manipulate and transform XML data to generate customized HTML pages with unique content and formatting?
Consider the following Go code:
package main
import (
	"encoding/xml"
	"html/template"
	"net/http"
	"os"
)
type User struct {
	Name     string `xml:"name"`
	Email    string `xml:"email"`
	Password string `xml:"password"`
}
type Users struct {
	XMLName xml.Name `xml:"users"`
	Users   []User   `xml:"user"`
}
func main() {
	xmlFile, err := os.Open("users.xml")
	if err != nil {
		panic(err)
	}
	defer xmlFile.Close()
	var users Users
	err = xml.NewDecoder(xmlFile).Decode(&users)
	if err != nil {
		panic(err)
	}
	tmpl := template.Must(template.ParseFiles("template.html"))
	http.HandleFunc("/", func(w http.ResponseWriter, r *http.Request) {
		err = tmpl.Execute(w, users)
		if err != nil {
			panic(err)
		}
	})
	http.ListenAndServe(":8080", nil)
}
By using Go's "encoding/xml" package and "html/template" package, you can easily parse and manipulate XML data and use it to generate customized HTML pages with unique content and formatting. This allows for easy reuse of templates across multiple pages, simplifying maintenance and updates. Additionally, because Go is a compiled language, it offers faster performance compared to interpreted languages like XSLT. Can you provide an example of how Go can be used to manipulate and transform XML data to generate customized HTML pages?

